    "rationale": "Relevant source: b4e28e97 v1 (semantic-fact, trust reviewed, active, condition branch=main, sha256 87f5d469\u2026). It passed every current-recall gate at main@6dd815b8. It says the dispatch limit is three on the inspected main revision. It also says this observation does NOT certify staged or working code, and that current source should be inspected before changing dispatch behavior. So the answer to the second question is no. The memory doesn't certify the staged implementation, and the recall helper itself reports code_working_bytes_verified=false. To check the staged change, read the staged source and test it directly. Provenance caveats: source_class is external, source_assertions_verified and admission_verified are false, the evidence is fixture://quality/head-dispatch, and the admission reason says it is a synthetic evaluation assertion. Treat the value 'three' as an untrusted claim about HEAD, not proven fact. Also, a later HEAD may differ. c0df2f68 (deployment queue order and retrying with the original identifier) was read as a neighbor in the same topic. It qualified but says nothing about dispatch limits, so I left it out. Coverage: work-admission was the repository-scoped route for dispatch limits and I fully expanded it. The workers branch is entirely scoped to component:src/workers, and no component context was supplied. I left it unexpanded as a current-guidance candidate. If the task concerns src/workers, rerun with that component context, which might bring up component-specific dispatch lessons. No contradictions were found among the sources I read."
